      <title>Trump FCC declares California isn't allowed to enforce phone service rules</title>
      <author>
                    Jon Brodkin
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/fcc-orders-california-to-stop-enforcing-phone-service-rules-against-att/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/fcc-orders-california-to-stop-enforcing-phone-service-rules-against-att/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">c146dd715631fbbbeff8a638e09642612a6fe857</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:28:24 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         FCC preempts state rules that require AT&amp;#038;T to keep offering home phone service.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/getty-att-logo-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="People at a conference walk by a large AT&amp;amp;T logo." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/getty-att-logo-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/getty-att-logo-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/getty-att-logo-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/getty-att-logo-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       AT&amp;T's stand at Mobile World Congress on February 27, 2023, in Barcelona, Spain.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       Getty Images | Joan Cros Garcia-Corbis                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;The Federal Communications Commission ordered California to stop enforcing its Carrier of Last Resort (COLR) rules against AT&amp;amp;T, setting up another battle between the Trump administration and California over the state’s right to enforce its own telecom laws and regulations.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;AT&amp;amp;T’s status as a Carrier of Last Resort in California requires it to offer telephone service to any potential customer in its territory. AT&amp;amp;T wants to stop offering basic phone service to new customers and &lt;a href="https://www.cpuc.ca.gov/industries-and-topics/internet-and-phone/att-colr-etc-proceedings/federal-applications-to-discontinue-landline-voice-service"&gt;discontinue service&lt;/a&gt; for 184,000 residential customers and 15,000 business customers on June 1, 2027.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.fcc.gov/document/fcc-removes-barriers-network-modernization-ca"&gt;order released today&lt;/a&gt; granted AT&amp;amp;T’s request to preempt state rules, following up on the FCC’s previous approval of AT&amp;amp;T’s petition to discontinue service. AT&amp;amp;T had tried to achieve the same result in court, but a federal &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/07/att-loses-key-ruling-in-bid-to-stop-offering-basic-phone-service-in-california/"&gt;judge rejected its request&lt;/a&gt; to override California’s rules in July.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The FCC order probably isn’t the last word, as California could sue the commission. California has stood alone against AT&amp;amp;T, which has received relief from COLR obligations in 20 of the 21 states in its wireline service territory.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Carr: California rules “designed for a bygone era”&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;“California’s Carrier of Last Resort framework may have made sense when it was established 30 years ago,” FCC Chairman Brendan Carr said in a statement released with today’s order. “But those rules were designed for a bygone era and no longer make sense in today’s competitive, advanced marketplace.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;If AT&amp;amp;T ends service on its old copper lines, many homes and businesses would not be offered modern fiber networks. AT&amp;amp;T claims a wireless home phone service that connects to the cellular network will be good enough, and the FCC agreed.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“The FCC has granted AT&amp;amp;T’s applications to replace legacy voice service with a modern, IP-based alternative in certain areas of the state. California’s state requirements are blocking AT&amp;amp;T from proceeding with a federally authorized transition,” Carr said.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.fcc.gov/ecfs/document/26109998546/1"&gt;California says&lt;/a&gt; its rules allow wireless replacements if they meet the state’s standards for basic phone service, but that AT&amp;amp;T must demonstrate that its wireless phone service would meet the COLR obligations. California’s requirements include flat-rate options for unlimited voice calls, discounted Lifeline rates for eligible low-income customers, and telephone relay service for people who are deaf and hard of hearing.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;h2&gt;Gomez: State officials know best&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;FCC Democrat Anna Gomez voted against the order, saying that state telecom commissions “are often the last line of defense for consumers. They know their communities, they take their complaints, and they answer to the people who rely on these services in a way that a federal agency in Washington cannot always match.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Gomez said the California Public Utilities Commission (CPUC) has an open proceeding on how to modernize its COLR rules, and that the FCC should let that process play out. Large technology transitions work best “when the state regulator is a partner with a real role,” but the FCC is treating the CPUC “as an obstacle to be cleared,” she said.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The CPUC “proceeding is the right place for California to weigh what a modern basic service requirement should look like, with input from the people who will actually be making that switch,” Gomez said. “AT&amp;amp;T has said it does not plan to discontinue service before June 1, 2027, which leaves time for the CPUC to finish its work. Rather than wait, this item tells the CPUC to write its new consumer protections around a federal ruling it opposed.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In March, the FCC issued an &lt;a href="https://docs.fcc.gov/public/attachments/FCC-26-19A1.pdf"&gt;order&lt;/a&gt; that made it easier for carriers to discontinue copper networks. The order asserted that state rules are subject to preemption if they conflict with the FCC’s discontinuance authorizations and authority. AT&amp;amp;T &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/05/att-sues-california-in-attempt-to-shut-off-old-phone-network/"&gt;cited the order&lt;/a&gt; in its lawsuit against California.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Trump officials and telecom companies have been trying to block California’s rules for years. The first Trump administration &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2019/10/why-ajit-pais-unhinged-net-neutrality-repeal-was-upheld-by-judges/"&gt;lost an attempt&lt;/a&gt; to preempt state net neutrality laws throughout the country in 2019, and &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2022/04/isps-cant-find-any-judges-who-will-block-california-net-neutrality-law/"&gt;California later defeated ISPs&lt;/a&gt; in a lawsuit that challenged its net neutrality law in court.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;California’s net neutrality law and other telecom regulations are again being threatened by rules the Trump administration applied to federal grants, but California &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/california-fights-preposterous-trump-admin-demand-to-scrap-state-broadband-laws/"&gt;rejected US demands&lt;/a&gt; to stop enforcing its laws this month. In that case, California may have to choose between enforcing its telecom laws and receiving $1.42 billion to deploy broadband to 270,571 locations.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>att</category>
      <category>california</category>
      <category>fcc</category>
