    <item>
      <title>Google rolls out new search homepage with AI chat history and no search button</title>
      <author>
                    Ryan Whitwam
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/googles-latest-search-page-redesign-drops-the-search-button-adds-ai-sidebar/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/googles-latest-search-page-redesign-drops-the-search-button-adds-ai-sidebar/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">c3f41a06e277f4f5c4384357dfcbc71b6d7868ed</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:58:29 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Google search is making it even easier to access AI tools on the search page.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/google-sign-logo-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="A large sign with Google&amp;#039;s &amp;quot;G&amp;quot; logo hangs outside during the daytime." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/google-sign-logo-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/google-sign-logo-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/google-sign-logo-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/google-sign-logo-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       A sign at the Google I/O Developers Conference in Mountain View, California on Tuesday, May 19, 2026.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       Getty Images | Bloomberg                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Google’s flagship search experience is evolving into an &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/05/buckle-up-google-is-set-to-remake-search-with-agentic-ai-in-2026/"&gt;AI experience&lt;/a&gt; with stunning speed. Even time-tested elements of search are getting AI makeovers, and the latest to get this treatment is the search homepage on Google.com. A redesign that’s been in testing for the last few weeks is now official, bringing easier access to AI chats and dropping the dedicated search button entirely.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Google search VP Robby Stein &lt;a href="https://x.com/rmstein/status/2123500235930640844"&gt;confirmed on X&lt;/a&gt; that Google is deploying the new desktop search page after a successful test. The page will now have a few more buttons under the search bar, all connected to AI features. There’s an image generation button, one for analyzing files, and one for brainstorming with Gemini. The “I’m Feeling Lucky” button remains, but the Google Search button that used to sit next to it is gone.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The search button has obviously been a part of Google’s search page forever. However, it wasn’t in a very convenient location in the modern interface. When you begin typing a query, the autocomplete suggestions cover that button. You have to click out of the text field to access the button, which is an extra step compared to simply mashing the Enter key to run your search.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;figure class="video ars-wp-video ars-wp-video--horizontal"&gt;   &lt;div class="" style=""&gt;     &lt;div class="wrapper ars-wp-video-wrapper relative"&gt;       &lt;video class="wp-video-shortcode absolute w-full h-full object-contain left-0 top-0" id="video-2183086-1" width="1080" height="1080" preload="metadata" controls="controls"&gt;&lt;source type="video/mp4" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/v-google-ai-mode-sidebar-search-GdxlOwec.mp4?_=1"&gt;&lt;/source&gt;The new AI-centric search homepage&lt;/video&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;      &lt;figcaption&gt;       &lt;span class="icon caption-arrow icon-drop-indicator"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;div class="caption font-impact dusk:text-gray-300 mb-4 mt-2 inline-flex flex-row items-stretch gap-1 text-base leading-tight text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-300"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-icon bg-[left_top_5px] w-[10px] shrink-0"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-content"&gt;       The new AI-centric search homepage            &lt;/div&gt;   &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;/figcaption&gt;   &lt;/div&gt; &lt;/figure&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The other big change brings your recent AI conversations to the forefront. If you’re logged into Google, the search homepage will have an expandable sidebar on the left with your recent AI Mode search threads. This sidebar will also be accessible on the results page. According to Stein, the sidebar made a “big difference” in how people made progress on their web searches, calling it a “simpler way to pick up where you left off.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;It’s easy to feel frustrated by changes like this if you don’t like Google’s new AI approach to search. But at the same time, the loss of functionality is minimal. Google probably has usage data that shows that particular search button was rarely used. Most people probably just hit Enter or click an autocomplete suggestion to go to the results.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;div class="ars-lightbox"&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="840" height="470" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-20-15_21_22-.png" class="fullwidth full" alt="" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-20-15_21_22-.png 840w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-20-15_21_22--640x358.png 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-20-15_21_22--768x430.png 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-20-15_21_22--384x216.png 384w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px"&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;               The current (old) Google search homepage.                               &lt;div class="ars-gallery-caption-credit"&gt;Credit:                    Credit:                                       Google                                   &lt;/div&gt;                           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;This change says more about Google’s priorities and the future of search. AI features get prominent buttons, and your AI conversations now have a whole sidebar UI. Meanwhile, traditional search is increasingly de-emphasized. There may come a day when AI Mode takes over completely, forcing you to manually select the old-fashioned version of search. It does feel like Google is inching in that direction.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>artificial-intelligence</category>
      <category>google-2</category>
      <category>search</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Meta reconsiders 2030 net-zero goal amid record emissions from AI data centers</title>
      <author>
                    Jeremy Hsu
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/meta-reconsiders-2030-net-zero-goal-amid-record-emissions-from-ai-data-centers/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/11/meta-reconsiders-2030-net-zero-goal-amid-record-emissions-from-ai-data-centers/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">c69d668bb3282112728b4bbb8a5e8db0a69e0a2c</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:47:10 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;       Meta is “re-evaluating” 2030 sustainability goals. Will others follow?     &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="8660" height="5773" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center.jpg" class="intro-image" alt="The $10 billion Meta El Paso Data Center is seen in the midst of construction on August 13, 2026 in El Paso, Texas." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center.jpg 8660w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center-640x427.jpg 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center-768x512.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center-2048x1365.jpg 2048w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center-980x653.jpg 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Meta-El-Paso-data-center-1440x960.jpg 1440w" sizes="(max-width: 8660px) 100vw, 8660px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       The $10 billion Meta El Paso Data Center is seen in the midst of construction on August 13, 2026 in El Paso, Texas.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/in-an-aerial-view-the-meta-el-paso-data-center-is-seen-on-news-photo/2290400797"               target="_blank"&gt;                       Brandon Bell/Getty Images                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Meta’s executives are rethinking the company’s original goal of achieving net-zero emissions by 2030. That revelation in Meta’s latest sustainability report comes as the company reported a record percentage increase in greenhouse gas emissions that exceeds any other Big Tech competitor racing to build new AI data centers.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The company’s &lt;a href="https://sustainability.atmeta.com/sustainability-report/"&gt;2026 sustainability report&lt;/a&gt; claims that “our long-term ambitions haven’t changed, but our operating environment has,” according to an introduction from Rachel Peterson, vice president of data centers at Meta, and Blair Swedeen, global head of net zero and sustainability at Meta. In the November 19, 2026, report, they attributed the changing conditions and the need for a rethink to how “demand for AI infrastructure has grown faster than almost anyone projected.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“We are re-evaluating our timelines, sequencing, and interim milestones—including the 2030 timeframe for our net zero emissions goal and our near-term intensity targets—so our approach reflects current operating realities while keeping progress transparent year over year,” Peterson and Swedeen wrote in the report.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Meta’s statement about re-evaluating its 2030 emissions goal is a significant public acknowledgment, given that Amazon, Google, and Microsoft have all stuck by their original sustainability goals so far.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But all the companies have acknowledged challenges in balancing their original sustainability goals against the breakneck buildout of &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/11/hyperscale-data-centers-use-2-of-us-electricity/"&gt;hyperscale data centers&lt;/a&gt;. Despite efforts to invest in more renewable energy projects, Meta and its rivals are still often pushing forward on AI data centers that rely on burning natural gas to generate electricity.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Rising emissions&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Meta’s report described the company’s total emissions as surging from 15.6 million metric tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent in 2024 to more than 21.7 million metric tonnes in 2025.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;That would make Meta’s annual carbon footprint &lt;a href="https://www.eia.gov/state/seds/data.php?incfile=/state/seds/sep_sum/html/rank_co2_capita.html&amp;amp;sid=US"&gt;roughly comparable&lt;/a&gt; to total emissions for the state of Idaho, while surpassing the total emissions of states such as Hawaii, Maine, South Dakota, and New Hampshire. The company attributed much of this emissions increase to “capital goods,” like as data center servers.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;That also represents a nearly 40 percent increase in Meta’s carbon footprint between 2024 and 2025, which is the highest percentage year-over-year increase for total emissions reported by any of the four major US tech giants.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;For comparison, &lt;a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/corporate-responsibility/topics/sustainability/report/"&gt;Microsoft reported&lt;/a&gt; a 25 percent increase in its overall carbon footprint, &lt;a href="https://sustainability.google/google-2026-environmental-report/"&gt;Google reported&lt;/a&gt; an 18 percent increase, and &lt;a href="https://sustainability.aboutamazon.com/2026-the-climate-pledge-report.pdf"&gt;Amazon’s report&lt;/a&gt; described a 16 percent increase between 2024 and 2025. However, Meta still likely has one of the smallest total carbon footprints among the Big Tech companies.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Meta’s &lt;a href="https://sustainability.atmeta.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Meta-2023-Environmental-Data-Methodology.pdf"&gt;approach to achieving net-zero emissions&lt;/a&gt; has included developing new renewable energy projects to match emissions and using carbon removal technology.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But even when factoring in such efforts, Meta still reported net emissions increasing from more than 8.1 million metric tonnes to more than 11.8 million metric tonnes for 2025. That represents a 45 percent increase.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Water woes&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;From a &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/06/when-it-comes-to-total-water-use-ai-data-centers-are-a-drop-in-the-bucket/"&gt;water consumption standpoint&lt;/a&gt;, Meta reported progress in reducing facility consumption from 825 million gallons in 2024 to 790 million gallons in 2025.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Yet that positive trend is tempered by the fact that Meta also described an increase in water consumption from “areas with water stress,” rising from 198 million gallons in 2024 to 246 million gallons in 2025.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Meta also reported a sharp increase in water consumption related to purchased electricity—the amount of water consumed by related power generation—from 19 billion gallons in 2024 to more than 26 billion gallons in 2025. This once again highlights that, for data centers, the far bigger water usage comes from the power plants that supply electricity to data centers as opposed to data centers directly using water for cooling.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Still, some minor credit is due. The publication &lt;a href="https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/meta-to-reevaluate-net-zero-timeline-on-the-back-of-record-emissions/"&gt;Data Center Dynamics&lt;/a&gt; pointed out that “Meta is the only hyperscaler to report indirect water use, a growing concern as data centers shift to closed-loop cooling systems that, despite using less water at source, require more electricity to run.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;It would very much be in the public interest for other Big Tech companies to follow suit on the reporting front—and public pressure continues to build after the &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/big-tech-claims-voters-ai-backlash-is-anti-trump-not-anti-tech/"&gt;AI backlash&lt;/a&gt; impact on US midterm election results.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>ai-data-centers</category>
      <category>big-tech</category>
      <category>carbon-emissions</category>
      <category>data-centers</category>
